RCP/M - INSTALLATION GUIDE

1. DOWNLOAD, UNPACK, RUN

Use the package matching your operating system and architecture. Unpack it
into a writable folder and keep all its files together. No configuration
is required for local use. Run the supplied launcher:

    macOS:  Start-RCPM.command
    Linux:  Start-RCPM.sh
    Windows: Start-RCPM.cmd
    Docker: use the supplied container instructions and persistent volume.

Defaults are direct TCP, local address 127.0.0.1, and caller port 4000.
The external RCPM.CONF describes these settings; editing it is optional.
First start creates private RBBS SYSOP credentials in SYSOP.DOC. Keep that
file private. Fresh packages contain no historical users or messages.

The package supplies the emulator, CP/M tools and virtual modem. No Python,
compiler, package manager or separately installed tcpser is needed to run.
macOS tools are unsigned; approve the identified application through macOS
Privacy & Security if required. Do not disable system-wide protection.

The local terminal is the sysop console. On successful listener creation:

    RCP/M is now running on port 4000.
    Listening address: 127.0.0.1 (direct raw TCP).
    See docs/INSTALLATION.DOC for information on how to connect
    from your system (Windows, Linux, macOS).

Direct mode displays 'RCP/M modem ready.' before answering. Virtual-modem
mode shows its Hayes initialization command and numeric 0 reply; allow a few
seconds for initialization, then connect. One caller at a time.
This foreground session must stay open while the service runs. There is no
tmux service, background daemon, automatic client launch or auto-reattach.

2. CONNECTING FROM WINDOWS, LINUX OR MACOS

Use your preferred terminal client with a RAW TCP connection to
127.0.0.1 port 4000. Select ANSI/VT100, 80 columns, and carriage-return
line endings. Press Return after connecting if the CP/M prompt does not
appear. The connection is byte-transparent; it is not a Telnet protocol
server. Select RAW rather than Telnet negotiation in the client.

Windows: PuTTY can use Connection type Raw, host 127.0.0.1, port 4000.
SyncTERM can use its Raw connection type on Windows, Linux and macOS.
Linux/macOS: 'nc 127.0.0.1 4000' is useful for a basic connection check;
a proper raw terminal client is preferable for file transfers and editing.
Clients are supplied/installed by the operator, not bundled with RCP/M.
Use the matching native host download; terminal clients are independent.

127.0.0.1 is local to the machine running RCP/M. For another computer to
connect, stop RCP/M, edit ADDRESS in RCPM.CONF to the Mac's LAN IPv4
address, restart, and allow that port in the host firewall as appropriate.
Connect clients to that LAN address. Raw TCP provides no encryption.

3. FIRST-START FILES AND ADMINISTRATION

The package supplies A:, I: and P:. P: includes all 310 SIGM and 93 CPMUG
libraries. The first start creates an empty J: mount disk and unique local
RBBS SYSOP password. SYSOP.DOC contains that PRIVATE generated password; it is
not the general manual and must not be shared with callers.

Read docs/SYSOP-GUIDE.DOC for routine operation, RBBS administration, uploads,
archive maintenance and backups. docs/DISK-IMAGES.DOC describes the raw image
formats and host editing tools. Keep the emulator stopped during host edits.

4. FIRST SHUTDOWN

BYE logs a caller off; it does not stop the server. To stop locally between
calls, press Ctrl-C, select Return at BYE's menu, wait for the CP/M prompt,
then enter SYSUTLS:BYEBYE. Full operating instructions are in docs/SYSOP-GUIDE.DOC.

5. OPTIONAL VIRTUAL MODEM (TCPSER / IP232)

Direct raw TCP passthrough is the default and requires no virtual modem.
Both compiled BYE5 drivers are in drivers/direct/ and drivers/ip232/.
The launcher installs the driver matching MODE before boot, while stopped.
Never replace the driver on a mounted/running A: disk.

tcpser is bundled. With RCP/M stopped, edit the commented external RCPM.CONF:

    MODE=ip232
    ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
    PORT=4000

Run the normal launcher. It starts tcpser, connects the emulator through a
private automatically chosen loopback port, and stops tcpser when CP/M exits.
PORT is always the CALLER port, in either mode. Do not start tcpser manually
or expose an internal modem endpoint. Restore MODE=direct and restart to
use direct TCP without tcpser. No other setting needs changing.

The native launcher accepts --mode, --address and --port for temporary
settings; --check reports configuration without starting the board. These
options do not edit RCPM.CONF. Advanced host integrations are documented separately.

6. PROVENANCE AND RELEASE STATUS

metadata/MANIFEST.json records the common payload and platform binary checksums.
metadata/ARCHIVE.sha256 lists all 403 P: libraries. metadata/VOLUMES.json records the curated
J: contents. Sources, patches and build notes are under source/. Original
notices are retained under docs/NOTICES/ and within the original libraries.
Component notices in docs/DISTRIBUTION-STATUS.DOC still apply. Fresh packages
do not import an operator's live disks. See the release evidence for tested hosts.

Microsoft M80/L80 and MicroPro WM (including WM.HLP) are not bundled as development tools.
Running this package does not require them. Builders supply their original
pinned toolchain disk separately; see source/BUILDING.DOC and
metadata/DEVELOPMENT-MEDIA.json. The DRI files remain on disks/library/offline/BUILD.dsk.
